WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.209 --> 00:00:02.500
JUSTIN BECKWITH: With almost
500 sessions in Next 2018,

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:04.720
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.940
So if you didn't see "Preventing
the Next Major Security

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.106
Breach," then stay tuned,
because here's the Rewind.

00:00:09.106 --> 00:00:11.926
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:18.390
No matter if your
company is big or small,

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:19.890
when it comes to
security, everyone

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:23.031
asks the same question--
am I secure enough?

00:00:23.031 --> 00:00:24.780
While it's easy to
focus on the technology

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.150
side of this question,
the pros know the truth.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:28.950
Security is a business problem.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.770
It comes down to trust and
balancing financial trade-offs.

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:34.450
There's also no perfect solution
to securing your business.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:36.690
And if someone tries to
sell you one, watch out.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:39.420
Instead, security prevention is
about addressing known factors

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.610
and tackling the most
frequent root causes--

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.849
known vulnerabilities
and container handling.

00:00:43.849 --> 00:00:45.390
Thinking about
vulnerabilities, there

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.300
are three primary places to
protect yourself-- code time,

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.190
build time, and deploy time.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:52.065
All three cases benefit
from better processes

00:00:52.065 --> 00:00:54.900
around handling your
code to reduce risk.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.664
This is fundamentally
a process problem,

00:00:56.664 --> 00:00:58.080
and your software
lifecycle can be

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.590
made safer with better process.

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:03.030
At code time, you
need a process to test

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:04.709
your third-party
libraries and tools

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:07.360
so you know you're integrating
safe code from the outside.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.210
At build time, you
need to address

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:11.920
issues in your operating
system and included packages

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:14.130
so you don't expose yourself
to a vulnerability based

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:15.307
on your platform.

00:01:15.307 --> 00:01:16.890
And at deploy time,
you need good ways

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.170
to ensure you're actually
deploying the right binary

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.720
so nobody can sneak in an
alternate dangerous version

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.089
without your knowledge.

00:01:23.089 --> 00:01:24.630
Luckily, Google has
some ways to make

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:26.427
all of this easier for you.

00:01:26.427 --> 00:01:29.010
Let's start with the Container
Registry Vulnerability Scanner,

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:31.320
which can examine your
containers before you deploy,

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.090
checking for any
known vulnerabilities

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:34.980
so you can patch your
update before sending

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:36.396
something unsafe out there.

00:01:36.396 --> 00:01:38.520
To keep your containers
safe once they're deployed,

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.860
it's key to install updates
and upgrades regularly--

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:42.540
preferably each day.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.060
And remove packages you
don't need to further reduce

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.290
your exposure.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:49.620
If possible, go find a smaller
OS distribution to even further

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:51.140
shrink your attack surface.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:53.640
Thankfully, that doesn't have
to be a manual process, adding

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.050
to your existing toil.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:57.630
We've got tools to help you
with integration and deployment

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:00.720
processes to improve security,
baking in scans and tests

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.090
to analyze what
you're deploying.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.910
And we'll notify you when any
new vulnerability is discovered

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:08.289
that affects your
code, via email,

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:12.060
web notifications, mobile
app, Pub/Sub, or API calls.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.586
Now, on the open source
side, the Grafeas project

00:02:14.586 --> 00:02:15.960
can help with your
security needs

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.810
by establishing a
consistent API for vendors

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:22.020
to define what artifacts
exist in a build system,

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:23.760
what vulnerabilities
might be present,

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.910
and what you can audit
throughout your lifecycle.

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:29.606
Google's container analysis
API implements Grafeas,

00:02:29.606 --> 00:02:31.230
and many other
companies have partnered

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.400
to strengthen this standard.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.030
What if you're using
Kubernetes already

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:37.860
and want to take advantage
of this vulnerability data?

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.540
The Kubernetes
Admission Controller

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:42.270
can sit between your code and
your Kubernetes environment,

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:44.340
intercepting requests
to the API and checking

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.281
the safety of the containers
before they're run.

00:02:47.281 --> 00:02:49.030
If you want to see it
in action, check out

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:52.140
Kelsey Hightower's demo linked
in the description below.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.240
Already we're making
good security progress,

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.550
but let's take it further.

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:57.750
What if you want to test
assertions before deploying

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:59.340
containers in your cluster?

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:01.740
Kritis, another open
source project we support,

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:05.100
enables you to prove build
provenance and require scanning

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.050
before container deployment.

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:10.830
Security is hard,
and we want to help.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.879
Google Cloud has many tools to
help you secure your software

00:03:13.879 --> 00:03:15.420
deployment process,
and we're working

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:18.180
closely with partners
across the space, openly

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:19.320
and collaboratively.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:20.230
Thanks for watching.

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:22.630
You can see the full talk
link in the description below.

